{"r2_ghost_tape_running_down","military",55,150,18,4,1,{"Bridge Tape","Attention - this bridge - is closed - by order","Dee","The batteries are going on it, you can hear the voice drop a little lower every loop, like somebody nodding off at his post","Bridge Tape","Turn - around - now","Marty","Whoever is behind that barrier hasn't said a word of his own in weeks - the tape does all his talking, that doesn't mean nobody's holding a rifle","Dee","So don't go up that bridge because a machine sounds tired - people who are tired shoot too","Bridge Tape","Final - warning"}},
{"r9_ins_d55_write_it_down","instruction",55,140,8,20,1,{"Marty","Get yourself a notebook, a composition book, a feed ticket pad, the back of an old checkbook - and start writing down what you did","Marty","Meals, supplies used, the road you took, who you saw - sounds like a chore, and it is","Marty","Here's why you'll do it anyway, two weeks back I'd have swore on my mother we had four gallons of bleach in this building - we had one, I was remembering the truck it came off of in June","Dee","When people get tired they quit remembering and start believing - I've caught myself at it, I write my hours down now or I will swear to you that I slept","Marty","And write the date on it, everybody in this county has lost a day already - I lost two, and the only reason I ever found them is that ledger","Dee","Put the notebook in the same place every night, same nail, same drawer, same shelf - a notebook you can't find is just a story you're telling yourself"}},
{"r5_host_labor_day_union","host",57,59,0,23,5,{"Marty","It's Labor Day weekend, I only know because the calendar on the wall has a combine on the September page","Dee","I worked every Labor Day for eleven years - holiday pay, time and a half","Marty","What do you figure you're making tonight?","Dee","Time and a half of nothing, minus a cup of your coffee - so I'm in debt","Marty","I'll be taking that up with the union","Dee","Marty, you're management and the union, you're also the janitor","Marty","And the janitor is on strike, some of you have seen the floor in here"}},
{"r9_host_d58_wrong_note","host",58,130,0,23,1,{"Marty","I've started writing notes to myself on feed tickets and setting them up by the mic, and this morning I read one out loud that wasn't meant for any of you","Marty","It said, ask Dee about the sleeping, I said that on the radio in the middle of the hog report","Dee","He did, and I am not going to talk about the sleeping","Marty","I'm tired in a way I don't have a word for yet - I'm not fishing for anybody to feel sorry for me - I'm telling you because when I get something wrong on here, I'd like you to know why I got it wrong","Marty","So if I say a road's clear and I meant a different road, come back at me - say, Marty, which road, make me say it twice","Dee","He'll say it twice, he's stubborn but he isn't proud"}},
{"r9_obit_d58_hollis_grain_scales","obituary",58,58,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, it's the Sunday of Labor Day weekend, and there's no work stopping anywhere in this county for it, so I won't make a point of that","Reverend Hollis","I've quit saying how many names before I read them - folks were counting the number instead of hearing the people","Reverend Hollis","Clell Gaddis, of March Ridge, who ran the scales at the grain elevator twenty-six years and could tell you whose truck was coming up the drive with his back to the window","Reverend Hollis","Verda Napier, of Muldraugh, who kept a coffee can of buttons and made about every girl on her street a doll dress out of a feed sack at some point in her life","Reverend Hollis","Claudie Ison, of the Ekron road, eighty-one years old, who out-worked men of fifty in that garden and had the wrists to show for it","Reverend Hollis","And a young man on the Riverside road that nobody has a name for - he had a class ring on, I wrote down the year and the initials and I'll read them at the end of every service until somebody claims him","Reverend Hollis","Service is at nine if you can get there, and at nine if you can't - Marty, I thank you for the chair"}},
{"r3_so_d59_labor_day","sign_on",59,59,5,9,8,{"Marty","Good morning, it's Monday, and according to the calendar on the wall with the tractor on it, it's Labor Day","Marty","The store would be closed today, I'd be out at the lake with a cooler and a lawn chair, and Nancy would be telling me to put on a hat","Marty","The store is closed today, I will not be at the lake, I am wearing a hat","Marty","Everybody take one thing off your list today - just one - leave it for tomorrow, that's our holiday - it's {time}"}},
{"r2_ghost_her_name_was_bernice","military",60,3650,7,17,1,{"Striker-12","Anybody on county fire, this is Striker-12, I'm leaving the overpass today - first time since July, over","Caller - Lorraine","Army? It's Lorraine, in West Point - where are you going?","Striker-12","Down to the dispatch office in Muldraugh, I want to see it - I talked to that lady every morning for two weeks and never once asked her name","Caller - Lorraine","It was Bernice - Bernice Hardesty, she went to my church - she did the altar flowers","Striker-12","Bernice, thank you, ma'am, this channel is yours now, somebody keep answering it - Striker-12, out"}},
{"r3_host_d60_curtis_couch","host",60,140,0,3,1,{"Dee","People ask me on nineteen if I've got anybody, I usually say, 'I've got all of you,' which is true, and is also a way of not answering","Dee","I've got a little brother - Curtis, he was a mechanic in the motor pool at Fort Knox - last time we talked was the Wednesday before, and he wanted to borrow my truck to move a couch","Dee","I told him no, because the last time he borrowed my truck, he brought it back with a couch still in it","Dee","So if anybody out there turns wrenches on Army trucks, and his name is Curtis Price, and he's got a laugh like a donkey, tell him he can have the truck"}},
{"r3_ins_d60_winter_list","instruction",60,100,5,12,1,{"Marty","Here's the winter list, from a man who's sold a lot of winter to a lot of farmers - start now, not when you can see your breath","Marty","Wood, more than you think, a house with one stove goes through about a cord a month when it's properly cold - dead standing wood is dry, anything lying on the ground is wet","Marty","Pipes, when it gets down in the twenties, drain the lines in any house you're not heating, or you'll lose the plumbing for good","Marty","Pick one room and make it the warm room, close off the rest - hang blankets over the doorways, everybody sleeps in the warm room, including whoever you're mad at","Dee","And hats, everybody sleeps in a hat, the babies especially - they lose heat faster than you do and they can't tell you"}},
{"r3_ins_d60_toothache","instruction",60,150,0,23,1,{"Dee","I've had more toothaches at the back door this month than broken bones - nobody has a dentist, and everybody's eating hard corn","Dee","Warm salt water - Swish it, spit it, several times a day, a little clove oil on a cotton ball against the tooth will numb it - no clove oil? Whole cloves out of somebody's spice rack, chewed a bit and packed beside it","Dee","If your face swells up, or you run a fever with it, that's an abscess, and it can get into your blood - that's antibiotic time, and it's come-see-me time","Dee","And don't let anybody talk you into pulling it with pliers after a few drinks - I've seen that go wrong twice this fall, and both men were very sure of themselves","Marty","One of them was me, I was very sure"}},
{"r3_wx_d60_walnut_hands","weather",60,90,5,9,1,{"Dee","Weather from the overnight, fog again on the river bottoms, thick - that's September for you, cool nights, warm ground, soup in all the low places","Dee","It'll lift by nine, high around eighty this afternoon - that's the nicest weather we've had since before, so enjoy it however you can","Marty","Leaves are turning on the poplars already, and the walnuts are dropping - if you're out walking, those green hulls will stain your hands black for a week - it's fine, it just makes you look like you've been up to something","Dee","Also, the ragweed is high, everybody on nineteen sounds like they've got a cold, it's not a cold - you're not dying - blow your nose"}},
{"r3_mb_d60_spelling_bee","music_bridge",60,150,10,16,1,{"Dee","No music today, so the church kids asked if they could have a spelling bee on the air - Marty said yes before I could stop him","Marty","I stand by it, first word, for Dustin, age nine - generator","Dustin","Generator - G, E, N, E, R, A, T, E, R. Generator","Dee","So close, it's an O near the end, honey, next word, for Brittany, age eleven - tomato","Brittany","Tomato - T, O, M, A, T, O. Tomato","Marty","Correct - Brittany takes the lead, we'll finish after the scanner notes - I'm told the final word is chrysanthemum, and I'm told I have to spell it first"}},
{"r3_sil_d60_numbers_bleed","silence",60,200,23,4,1,{"99.6 FM","(Static, under it, some other station bleeding through, a man reading numbers slowly in a flat voice - then nothing)"}},
{"r5_trade_teacher_adele_lessons","trade",60,250,9,16,1,{"Caller - Adele","My name is Adele Pruett, I taught third grade in March Ridge for thirty-one years","Caller - Adele","I'm looking for children's books - Readers, picture books, arithmetic workbooks, anything with pages","Marty","What are you offering, Miss Pruett?","Caller - Adele","Lessons - bring me any child between five and twelve, and I'll teach them to read and do their sums - bring a can of something for my lunch, if you can spare it","Caller - Adele","The March Ridge school library, ten in the morning, weekdays - come to the side window and knock, I've nailed the front doors shut","Dee","Everybody, that is the best trade we've had on this station, and I'm including the insulin","Marty","I'll bring you my old seed catalogs, Miss Pruett, they've got real nice pictures of corn","Caller - Adele","Marty Colburn, you were in my class in nineteen forty-seven, and I'm still waiting on your book report"}},
{"r5_trade_wedding_dress_kathy","trade",60,250,9,16,1,{"Caller - Kathy","Marty, Dee, I'm getting married","Dee","Kathy! Congratulations, honey","Caller - Kathy","His name is Mike, we met in a church basement in Rosewood in July - there's a preacher at our camp who'll do it","Caller - Kathy","I don't need a real wedding dress - anything white - a nightgown, a tablecloth, I don't care","Marty","What have you got to trade?","Caller - Kathy","Two cases of motor oil, ten-W-thirty - it's Mike's, he says it's fine","Dee","Somebody out there has a white dress hanging in a closet they'll never open again - call in - Kathy, where can they find you?","Caller - Kathy","The Riverside Methodist church lot, one in the afternoon, I'll be there every day this week","Marty","Mike is giving up his motor oil for this, folks - in Knox County, that's love"}},
{"r5_joke_sid_crossword_louisville","caller_joke",60,300,17,22,1,{"Caller - Sid","Marty, I found a crossword book in a house off Louisville Road - every puzzle already done, in pen","Marty","That's a shame, Sid","Caller - Sid","No, that's the good part, he got nearly every answer wrong - in pen, with total confidence","Caller - Sid","Capital of Kentucky, nine letters, he wrote Louisville and squeezed the last E into the margin","Caller - Sid","So I've been going through fixing all of his, most fun I've had since July, feels like I made a friend who's an idiot"}},
{"r5_trade_cigarettes_jimmy","trade",60,250,9,16,1,{"Caller - Jimmy","Marty, I need cigarettes bad - any kind, I've got a full propane tank off a gas grill","Dee","Jimmy, I'm going to give you something for free before you trade that tank away","Caller - Jimmy","Here it comes","Dee","You've been without since July, your body's already done the worst of quitting, and you're about to undo it for a pack of stale smokes - that tank cooks your food for weeks","Caller - Jimmy","Dee, with respect, I didn't call the nurse, I called the Trading Post","Marty","Jimmy, you called both, we come as a set","Caller - Jimmy","Fine, anybody with cigarettes, the gas station at the north end of Muldraugh, two o'clock - and anybody with willpower, I'll take some of that too"}},
{"r5_trade_wallet_crabtree","trade",60,300,9,16,1,{"Caller - Vicky","This isn't a trade, exactly, I found a wallet in a car on 31W, there's a driver's license in it for a Dennis Crabtree, of Brandenburg","Caller - Vicky","There's forty dollars in it, for whatever that's worth now - and school pictures - three kids, the ones with the laser background","Marty","What would you like to do with it, Vicky?","Caller - Vicky","Give it back, if Dennis is out there, or his wife, or anybody who knew him - I don't want anything for it","Caller - Vicky","The flagpole in front of Irvington high school, noon, any day this week - I'll have it in a sandwich bag so the pictures stay dry","Marty","Dennis Crabtree, of Brandenburg, the Irvington high school flagpole, at noon, if you know that name, pass it along"}},
{"r5_trade_welder_dale_window_bars","trade",60,300,9,16,1,{"Caller - Dale","Marty, this is Dale, I welded for the county road department eighteen years, I've got a welder, a generator, and about forty feet of rebar","Caller - Dale","I'll put bars on your windows, two windows for five gallons of gas, since that's what it takes to run the rig","Marty","Is it loud, Dale?","Caller - Dale","The welding's not so bad, the grinding is, I'll do it in the middle of the day, and you'll want somebody watching the road while I work","Dee","And Dale, wear a proper helmet, I've treated enough flash burn for one lifetime - it feels like sand in your eyes for two days","Caller - Dale","Yes ma'am, the old weigh station on 31W, south of Muldraugh, eight in the morning - tell me how many windows and what size","Marty","The weigh station on 31W, eight AM, measure your windows before you go, folks - Dale is not driving out twice"}},
{"r7_mil_ghost_sitrep_hour","military",60,250,5,10,1,{"Cardinal-1-6","Cardinal Main, this is Cardinal-1-6 with the morning SITREP, over","Cardinal-1-6","Line one, personnel - one, line two, ammunition - nine rounds, line three, water - the creek, over","Cardinal-1-6","Line four, rations, whatever's in the houses on this road - line five, vehicles, none that run, over","Cardinal-1-6","Line six, communications, this set, and a car battery I charge off a windmill a farmer let me use, over","Cardinal-1-6","Line seven, mission capable, I'm still stood where you put me - take that however you want to take it, over","Cardinal-1-6","Cardinal Main, that's the report for today, same time tomorrow - Cardinal-1-6, out"}},
{"r3_so_d62_fifty_four_degrees","sign_on",62,75,5,8,1,{"Marty","Good morning, something happened overnight that I want to report as news - it got down to fifty-four degrees","Marty","I stood out on the loading dock in a flannel shirt holding a cup of hot water with a twice-used tea bag in it, and I felt like a human being for about four minutes","Marty","Then I remembered what cool mornings mean is coming - wood - blankets, windows that shut - so that's the news and the homework in one","Marty","It's {weekday}, {date}, {time}, ninety-nine point six, March Ridge - the maple out front has started turning at the top"}},
{"r3_host_d62_letter_in_ledger","host",62,100,19,23,1,{"Marty","Dee said I ought to write Nancy a letter, she says she's seen families do it at the hospital, for folks who couldn't hear anymore - you don't mail it, you just write it","Marty","So I did, I'm not going to read it to you, most of it's none of your business","Marty","I'll read you one line, because I laughed when I wrote it - 'the books are a mess, I can't figure out which column the rooster goes in'","Marty","Anyway, I folded it up and put it in the green ledger - right after July the eighth","99.6 FM","(A short quiet)","Marty","Classifieds after this"}},
{"r9_obit_d62_caller_guard_socks","obituary",62,200,8,21,1,{"Caller - Zelma","Marty, my boy was with the Guard unit that went up to the bridge - Delmer Yount, he was nineteen","Marty","I'm listening, Miz Zelma","Caller - Zelma","I don't have one thing to tell you about the bridge - what I've got is a card he mailed me from basic in ninety-two that says the food's fine and please send socks","Caller - Zelma","That's all it says, food's fine, send socks, love Delmer - I sent the socks and I never did hear whether they fit him","Marty","You want him read","Caller - Zelma","I want him read and I want it said that he was nineteen and he wrote his mother for socks - people are going to talk about them boys at the bridge for a long time and they won't know any of that about him","Marty","Delmer Yount, nineteen years old - wrote home for socks, that's on the list now and it stays on the list"}},
{"r9_trade_d63_no_gun_trades","trade",63,140,9,16,1,{"Marty","I've got a note here offering a milk cow in calf for a rifle, and I'm going to tell you what I'm doing with that note, which is nothing","Marty","I have read four gun trades on this radio, two went fine - one I don't know about, and one went to a man I would not hand a rifle to if he was the last man in Kentucky, and I handed it to him by reading a piece of paper out loud","Dee","He's been chewing on this for three days","Marty","So no guns or ammo on this station anymore, not even a box of shells for eggs - do that with people you know by name","Marty","I know what that costs some of you, I know a woman by herself may need one worse than I need a clean conscience - I don't have a good answer for her, I've got this one, and it's the only one I've got","Marty","The cow stands, somebody trade this man something for a milk cow in calf - she's a Jersey and he says she's gentle, which is what every man says about his cow"}},
{"r9_ins_d64_dog_rules","instruction",64,150,8,20,1,{"Marty","Dogs, a lot of you have took in a dog that wasn't yours, and there's a useful part to this and a hard part, and I'm going to do both","Marty","Useful part, a dog hears them before you do and smells them a long way off - learn your dog's tell - Wendell's is his back, right along the shoulder, before he makes any sound at all","Dee","Learn the difference between his deer bark and the other one - you will, takes about a week and after that you'll never mistake it again as long as you live","Marty","Hard part, never let a dog run at them - not to defend you, not to drive them off, not one time - a dog that gets hold of one is a dog you have got to deal with, and you know what I'm saying","Marty","So a rope, or a recall, and I don't mean a dog that comes when he isn't busy - tie him at night, feed him where you can watch the road while you do it","Dee","And a dog will drink out of anything, that is your dog's entire plan for water - keep his bowl full so he doesn't go find worse","Marty","Wendell is presently asleep on the cord that runs to the transmitter, which he is not supposed to do and knows it"}},
{"r9_mb_d66_whistled_tune","music_bridge",66,145,0,23,1,{"Marty","A woman called in and asked could she do the music tonight - I said we don't have music, she said she knew that","Caller - Cordie","I can't sing a lick and I won't, my daddy whistled and I can whistle just like him - is that all right?","Marty","Go ahead, I'm holding this up to the speaker so everybody gets it","Caller - Cordie","That's it, that's all of it - he'd do that coming up the porch steps so Mama would know it was him and not somebody else","Marty","Cordie, you can have this spot any night you want it","Caller - Cordie","I might take Tuesdays, I'd have to practice"}},
{"r3_sf_d70_stove_season","sign_off",70,110,22,1,1,{"Marty","Nights are getting cool, and a lot of you are lighting stoves and fireplaces that haven't been lit in years - please listen to this one before you go to bed","Marty","Look up the chimney in daylight before you light it - birds build in them all summer, a stopped-up flue fills a house with smoke while everybody's asleep","Dee","And a charcoal grill indoors, or a generator running in the garage, will kill everybody in the house in their sleep - no noise, no smell, I have seen it this fall - don't","Marty","Count your people, check your locks, and crack a window an inch - good night"}},
{"r3_host_d72_hollis_ten_strokes","host",72,72,9,11,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, some of you asked why we don't ring the church bell on Sundays anymore - you know why, I'll say it for the children listening - it calls them, too","Reverend Hollis","So here's what we do instead, at ten o'clock, wherever you are, stop and be still for as long as it takes a bell to ring ten times - count it in your head if you want","Reverend Hollis","We've done it at the church three weeks now, fourteen people standing in a room, not ringing a bell - it's the loudest thing I ever heard","Reverend Hollis","It's about ten now, I'll be quiet with you","99.6 FM","(Quiet, for about as long as ten strokes of a bell)","Reverend Hollis","Thank you - good morning"}},
{"r9_sf_d75_woodpile_count","sign_off",75,125,21,23,1,{"Marty","Last thing, tomorrow go out and count your wood, don't look at it, count it, ricks or rows, write the number down","Marty","I counted mine Sunday and found out I've got about five weeks of heat and a hundred and some days of winter - that is the kind of arithmetic that does a man good","Dee","He came in from counting and sat down in the chair and didn't say anything for a good while","Marty","So tomorrow I'm going to cut wood, that's the plan, plain as that - cut wood, say your names in the morning, cut wood - count your people - good night"}},
{"r9_so_d78_first_frost_warning","sign_on",78,115,6,9,1,{"Marty","Morning, forty-one degrees on the bin and the fog is laying in the bottoms like somebody spilled it - it's {time}","Marty","This is the week I'd be selling cover crop seed and telling everybody frost is about three weeks out - frost is about three weeks out, that part didn't change","Marty","So today's the day to walk your water lines and find out where they're going to freeze - not in November when they do it, today, while you can stand out there in a shirt","Dee","And get a hat on the little ones in the mornings - I'm going to say that four hundred times between now and March, so you may as well get used to it","Marty","Go on and start your day, and hats on the little ones"}},
{"r9_obit_d79_hollis_lost_and_found","obituary",79,79,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Hollis here, in a feed store on a Sunday, and no, I'm not apologizing for it anymore","Reverend Hollis","Alma Renfro, of West Point, who kept the lost and found at the elementary school thirty-one years - she washed every coat before she gave it back and she never once said whose fault it was","Reverend Hollis","Tilman Sizemore, of Rosewood, a tobacco man with a bad hip, who still climbed up in the barn to hang the top tier because he didn't trust another man's knots","Reverend Hollis","Dorcas Ledford, of March Ridge, who called the prayer chain every Wednesday for twenty years - when she got to the end of the list she'd start it over and call the ones she thought had sounded tired","Reverend Hollis","And Ruel Eldridge's grandson Bobby, who was nine","Reverend Hollis","I'm not going to say anything about Bobby except that his grandad asked me to read him and I read him - Ruel, we're all thinking about your house this morning","Reverend Hollis","The young man with the class ring is still unclaimed - the year is nineteen eighty-eight and the initials are a D and a W"}},
{"r3_sf_d80_three_seconds","sign_off",80,120,0,2,1,{"Dee","There's somebody who keys up on nineteen every night around one-thirty - doesn't say a word, holds it about three seconds and lets go - been going on since September","Dee","I don't know who you are, I've decided you're checking in - so - I'm here, everybody else, that was for them","Dee","If you ever want to say something, I'll be right here - if you don't, that's alright too, three seconds is plenty","Dee","Good night, ninety-nine point six is off the air"}},
{"r3_sil_d80_gate_banging","silence",80,150,22,4,1,{"99.6 FM","(Wind across the microphone, the back door of the store, left open, knocking softly against its stop)","Dee","Sorry, I went out to shut the gate - it's shut, the back door's shut now too - everything's fine"}},
{"r5_host_wendell_fly_bait","host",80,300,0,23,1,{"Dee","Wendell got into the fly bait last night","Marty","He's fine, he threw it up behind the seed potatoes, which is about where I'd have done it too","Dee","The point is, put your poisons up high, rat bait, fly bait, antifreeze, dogs and toddlers don't read labels","Dee","Antifreeze tastes sweet to them, a dog will lap it right off a driveway - clean up your spills","Marty","Wendell has been written up, he doesn't seem troubled by it"}},
{"r5_joke_wade_rosebush","caller_joke",80,300,18,23,1,{"Caller - Wade","Hey, Dee, quick one - I got scratched pruning my mama's rosebush this morning","Dee","Wash it with soap and water, Wade","Caller - Wade","Did that, and I'm about ninety percent sure it was the rosebush, hahaha","Dee","Wade - what's the other ten percent?","Caller - Wade","Heh, the other ten percent is why I called tonight instead of tomorrow - anyway - how's that dog of yours?","Marty","Wendell's fine, Wade, he's right here by my foot"}},
{"r9_trade_d84_glasses_swap_meet","trade",84,165,9,16,1,{"Dee","This one is mine, everybody go home and find the eyeglasses that aren't yours - the pair in the drawer, the pair in the truck, the pair on a dead relative's nightstand - all of them","Dee","I want them in a box on the counter at Colburn Feed - frames don't matter, scratched don't matter, we are matching strength, not looks","Marty","Tell them the holding-it-out part","Dee","If you have to hold a page out at arm's length to read it, you need reading glasses, and whatever pair lets you read is the right pair - try all of them, it takes ten minutes and it costs nothing","Dee","Far away is a different animal, if the road signs have gone soft on you, I can't fix that out of a box, and I won't pretend I can","Marty","Saturday, out front, nine to noon - bring glasses, take glasses - bring a chair, too, because she'll keep you standing there a while","Dee","And if a pair works, put your name on the case in pencil - somebody may need to know what strength you are some day when you can't tell them"}},
{"r9_ins_d86_cutting_alone","instruction",86,175,8,20,1,{"Marty","Firewood, I know you've cut all your life - so had both the men I know of who got hurt cutting this month","Caller - Garnett","Marty, it's Garnett, I cut pulpwood nine years, the thing that gets a man in a hurry is a dead limb hung up over his head - look up before you start the saw - look up twice","Caller - Garnett","Second thing's the pinch - Tree's leaning, you cut straight through, the cut closes on your bar, and now you've got a running saw stuck up over you","Marty","Which is not a thing a man fixes by himself","Caller - Garnett","You can't fix nothing by yourself, but everybody's cutting by theirself now, so here's what I'd say - tell somebody where you're at and when you'll be back, and make them come look for you","Dee","A saw cut in a leg is minutes, not hours - if you are going to cut alone, cut alone with a belt in your pocket and the truck close enough to get to","Marty","And that saw is loud, so cut mornings, an hour not four, and put somebody on watch with empty hands"}},
{"r9_host_d88_man_who_says_nothing","host",88,160,0,23,1,{"Marty","There's somebody keys up on nineteen most mornings and doesn't talk - you hear the click, then you hear a room, then you hear the click again","Marty","I know what a man keying a mic and not talking sounds like, because I did it - first four days I had this thing I did it about a dozen times","Dee","He's got a clock in the room with him, you can hear it ticking if you turn it up","Marty","So, good morning to whoever that is, you don't have to say a word - I'll keep saying good morning and you keep clicking and we'll call that a conversation, because that's what it is","Marty","And if you ever do want to say something, there's no hurry on it - I'm here at seven"}},
{"r3_ins_d90_store_rules","instruction",90,150,0,23,1,{"Marty","A lot of you have groups now, ten, twenty people - some of you asked how we handle strangers at the store, so here's the store's way - use what fits","Marty","Nobody comes in after dark - nobody, you sleep in your truck in the lot where we can see you, and in the morning we talk","Dee","Anybody new gets looked over, arms, legs, neck, back - in a room with a door, and a woman in the room if it's a woman - it's not personal, I've been looked over too","Marty","They keep their weapon, but it stays in the office with a tag on it till they leave - nobody's ever argued that twice","Marty","And they eat before we ask them anything, a hungry man tells you whatever he thinks gets him fed - a fed man tells you the truth, mostly"}},
{"r5_trade_travis_firewood_labor","trade",90,180,9,16,1,{"Caller - Travis","My name's Travis, I don't have much to trade except a strong back","Caller - Travis","I'll split and stack firewood for two days for anybody with a winter coat and boots that fit - I wear a large and a size eleven","Dee","Travis, how old are you?","Caller - Travis","Nineteen","Dee","Travis, eat breakfast before you swing an axe for anybody - I mean that","Marty","Where can folks find you?","Caller - Travis","The low-water bridge at Echo Creek, seven in the morning - I'll wait till nine","Marty","Echo Creek low-water bridge, seven AM, and whoever takes him up on it, feed the boy"}},
{"r5_trade_roundup_fall_notes","trade",90,140,9,16,1,{"Marty","We've got a stack of notes that came through the mail slot, so I'm going to read the Trading Post straight through - pencils ready","Marty","A woman on Old Ekron Road has a woodstove, cast iron, no cracks - you haul it, she wants a hog, or half a hog - Ekron post office, noon","Dee","A man in West Point has four tires off a half-ton pickup with decent tread - he wants antibiotics, and no, he is not getting them through this station","Marty","He'll also take wool blankets - West Point town hall parking lot, ten AM","Marty","Somebody in Riverside is offering a hundred and twenty jars of home-canned tomatoes for a shotgun - Ma'am, you know the rule - call back with something else","Dee","The Brandenburg church ladies are trading hand-knit socks for kerosene - two pair a gallon - Brandenburg riverfront park, one PM","Marty","And someone left a note that just says, 'Have ferrets - will trade ferrets.' No name, no place, whoever you are, I have questions","Dee","That's the Trading Post, meet in daylight, bring somebody, and count what you got before you drive off"}},
{"r7_mil_ghost_relay_two_unmanned","military",90,300,0,23,1,{"Relay Two","Any station, any station, this is Relay Two on the knob - I am a relay, I have no unit and no orders and no traffic to pass","Relay Two","If you can hear me and you cannot hear somebody else, tell me who you want to reach and I will call them for you all day","Relay Two","I've got a generator, about forty gallons, and a notebook with sixty-one names in it of people looking for people","Relay Two","I read the whole notebook out loud at noon and at six - two of them got answered, two out of sixty-one, and that's worth the diesel","Relay Two","Relay Two is up from first light till dark - come up on this frequency and just start talking, I'll find whoever you're after","Relay Two","Relay Two, standing by"}},
{"r3_host_d93_hollis_piano_lid","host",93,93,9,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","I have to tell you that Ada Ogle passed on Thursday - some of you knew her, more of you took piano lessons from her and wished you hadn't","Reverend Hollis","Not them, her heart - in her own bed, at eighty, which is more than most of us get now, and she knew it","Reverend Hollis","She asked for one thing, she asked us not to close the lid on her piano - she said a closed piano looks like a coffin, and she'd seen enough of those","Reverend Hollis","So the lid's up, it's in the fellowship hall, open, and nobody playing it - if you come by, you can press a key - softly, she'd tell you to fix your wrists"}},
{"r3_wx_d95_pick_the_green_ones","weather",95,115,5,10,1,{"Marty","Weather - clear skies overnight, and clear in October means cold, thirty-four on the grain bin at five this morning, and frost in the low spots","Marty","Tonight will be colder, if you've still got tomatoes on the vine, pick every one today, green or not - wrap the green ones in newspaper, and they'll ripen on the counter","Marty","Cover what's left in the garden with old bedsheets tonight - pull them off in the morning","Marty","Turnips and collards don't care, collards get sweeter after a frost - the old folks were right about that one, and I intend to eat my own weight in them"}},
{"r5_host_first_frost_knee","host",95,110,0,23,1,{"Marty","Frost on the truck windshield this morning, the first one","Dee","He came in rubbing his knee like it owed him money","Marty","This knee has forecast the weather since the seventies - it's more reliable than anything left on the dial","Dee","And what does the knee say?","Marty","The knee says we should have started cutting firewood in August","Dee","The knee is right, everybody, start cutting now, stack it up off the ground and keep the top covered"}},
{"r3_wx_d96_ladybugs","weather",96,112,5,10,1,{"Marty","Weather, we've got one of those warm spells October throws in just to be mean - seventy-five this afternoon, and ladybugs all over the south side of every building","Marty","The old folks called days like this a gift, they also said a warm spell this late means a hard winter behind it - they said a lot of things","Marty","Use it, air out your bedding - get the wood split - wash whatever you've been putting off washing, including yourself, and I'm looking at nobody in particular","Dee","He's looking at the fellow from the Ekron road","Marty","I'm looking at nobody, high of seventy-five - frost again by the weekend"}},
{"r9_host_d96_sign_on_31w","host",96,175,0,23,1,{"Marty","Somebody painted a sign and nailed it to the county line marker on 31W - white house paint on a piece of plywood, it says NINETY NINE POINT SIX IF YOU'RE ALONE","Marty","Two fellows told me about it before I drove up there and looked at it myself, which I had no business doing, and Dee has already said her piece about that","Dee","I said my piece","Marty","I sat in the truck and looked at it a while - I don't know how a man's supposed to feel about being a landmark - I sell hog feed","Marty","But here's what I decided on the way home, whoever painted that spelled the numbers out in words, so somebody who's never touched a dial could find it - that is a kind thing to have thought of"}},
{"r3_sf_d100_doorframe_tally","sign_off",100,100,22,23,8,{"Marty","Dee informs me that today is the hundredth day since the ninth of July - she's been keeping a tally on the office doorframe with a pocketknife","Dee","It's a very neat tally","Marty","It is very neat, groups of five - I wanted to say something wise about a hundred days, and all I've got is that I thought this would be over in three","Marty","Count your people, put a mark on whatever you've been counting, good night"}},
{"r3_mb_d100_irene_fuses","music_bridge",100,150,9,17,1,{"Marty","Before we go on, I've got a visitor - Lloyd Tipton's wife, Irene, walked over from her daughter's this morning to see what I'd done with his radio","Irene","He'd have hated the tape job on that microphone, Marty","Marty","Yes ma'am","Irene","He kept spare fuses for it in the hall closet - I brought them, and don't touch the knob that says 'DON'T'","Marty","Irene, while I've got you - what does the 'DON'T' knob do?","Irene","Lord, I never asked - thirty-eight years, I figured it was safer not to know"}},
{"r5_trade_harold_piano_supper","trade",100,300,9,16,1,{"Caller - Harold","Marty, I've got an upright piano - free, you haul it","Marty","Harold, nobody in Knox County is hauling a piano anywhere right now","Caller - Harold","I figured, so here's what I'll trade instead - somebody come play it - one time, I'll cook supper","Marty","Just play it?","Caller - Harold","It hasn't been played since June, it's in tune - I had the man out in May","Caller - Harold","Four Hollow Road, Irvington - come in the daylight, knock twice and holler your name good and loud, so I know you're not one of them","Marty","Folks, that one's not a trade, but if I could play, I'd go"}},
{"r5_joke_pauline_upper_plate","caller_joke",100,300,17,22,1,{"Caller - Pauline","Marty, I have lost my teeth","Dee","Pauline, are you hurt?","Caller - Pauline","Not those teeth, honey, my upper plate, I took it out to rinse it at the creek, set it on a fence post, and walked off without it","Caller - Pauline","Somewhere between Ekron and the river, if you find a set of dentures on a fence post, they're mine - don't try them on","Marty","Pauline, why on earth would anybody try them on?","Caller - Pauline","Marty, it's been months, people are trying on everything"}},
{"r9_obit_d100_hollis_mower_full_tank","obituary",100,100,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, it's cold in here - Marty's got the one stove going, and I've got my coat on over the good jacket, which nobody can see, which is the first time in nineteen years that's been true","Reverend Hollis","Ercel Hood, of Ekron, who mowed the churchyard at Little Bethel eleven years and would not take gas money for it - that mower's in my shed with a full tank in it - I can't bring myself to run it down","Reverend Hollis","Icie Mullins, of Muldraugh, ninety-four, born in a house with no electric in it and died in a house with no electric in it, and made the same joke about that twice","Reverend Hollis","Lonzo Dunn, of West Point, who went out after somebody else's child, and got the child home","Reverend Hollis","I'll say that part again, he got the child home, everything that happened after is between that man and the Lord, and it is nobody's business on a radio","Reverend Hollis","The boy with the class ring has a name now - his mother heard it said in Valley Station, I'm not going to read it this morning - I'm going to go sit with her first"}},
{"r9_joke_d105_courthouse_parking","caller_joke",105,180,16,22,1,{"Caller - Ottis","Marty, I've got good news and bad news","Marty","Let's have the good","Caller - Ottis","I finally got the parking spot right out front of the courthouse - the one by the door, eleven years I've wanted that spot","Marty","And the bad?","Caller - Ottis","There's nothing in the courthouse","Marty","There is not","Caller - Ottis","I parked there anyway, sat in the truck about ten minutes with my arm out the window like a man with business"}},
{"r9_trade_d108_boy_inhalers","trade",108,185,9,16,1,{"Caller - Delma","Marty, my boy is nine and he's asthmatic and I have got two inhalers left and I don't know how to say this part so I'm just going to say it","Marty","Say it how it comes","Caller - Delma","I'll trade anything in this house - anything, I've got a sewing machine, a deep freezer, a wedding set and a pickup that runs","Dee","Delma, before you offer the truck - how many puffs is he taking a day, and is he taking them because he's bad or because he's scared?","Caller - Delma","Some of both, I couldn't tell you which anymore","Dee","Then here's what we do - bring him here to me and let me listen to him and count with you, and we will get you a number you can plan against - a canister has got more in it than the fear does","Dee","And everybody listening - go look in your medicine cabinets and your dead relatives' bathrooms for an inhaler - check the bottom for a date and bring it anyway if it's old","Marty","Don't nobody trade her a thing for them, bring them to Colburn Feed and I'll write your name in the book, and if it's ever you that needs something, I'll read your name first"}},
{"r5_host_halloween_taffy","host",112,114,0,23,5,{"Dee","Tonight would have been trick-or-treat","Marty","Every year I set a bowl of those peanut butter taffies on my counter, the ones in the orange and black wax paper - nobody ever wanted them, I bought them every year","Dee","Nobody's coming to the door in a mask this year","Marty","And if anybody does come to your door tonight, you don't open it - costume or no costume","Dee","Kids, if you're out there, dress up anyway - inside - show your mama - tell her Dee said so","Marty","I've also got forty pounds of that taffy left, is the other thing I'm saying - it doesn't go bad, I'm not sure it was ever good"}},
{"r9_ins_d112_chimney_fire","instruction",112,190,8,20,1,{"Marty","Everybody's burning wood now, even folks who never did, chimney fires are coming - let's talk before yours","Marty","Green wood and a slow smoky fire builds creosote in the pipe - it looks like shiny black tar - burn hot for twenty minutes once a day and you build a lot less of it","Caller - Willard","Marty, I was on the fire department at West Point nine years - a chimney fire sounds like a freight train in your wall - once you hear it you won't ask anybody what it was","Caller - Willard","Shut the air off, damper down, door shut, choke it - then get everybody out of the house and go outside and look at your roof","Marty","Not water?","Caller - Willard","Not up the pipe, water in a hot flue will crack it and then you've got fire in the wall - choke it and watch the roof, and if the roof goes you're done, so get the people out first","Caller - Willard","And clean it, a sack of chain on a rope, pulled through from the top, twice a winter - you'll be filthy and you'll be alive","Dee","And if you wake up dizzy with a headache in a closed-up house, that's not the flu - get out in the air first and argue about it after"}},
{"r3_so_d114_clocks_back","sign_on",114,114,5,8,8,{"Marty","Good morning, it's Halloween - it's also the last Sunday in October, which means the clocks go back an hour","Marty","I gave a lot of thought to whether we should bother - I decided yes, because Dee needs something to be right about today","Dee","The clocks go back, everybody set your clocks back one hour, if you don't have a clock, you're fine, carry on","Marty","So it's an hour earlier than you think, and it's {time}, whatever that means now - nobody's handing out candy on the road this year - Reverend Hollis will be on shortly"}},
{"r3_host_d114_hollis_hard_candy","host",114,114,9,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Happy Halloween - I know, some of you think a preacher oughtn't say that, my mother made me a ghost costume out of a flour sack in nineteen twenty-six, and I turned out mostly alright","Reverend Hollis","The children at the church wanted to dress up this year, and their parents didn't know what to tell them - I said let them","Reverend Hollis","So we've got two cowboys, a princess in a lace tablecloth, and one little girl who wants to be a nurse, because of Dee","Dee","Oh, no, don't do that to me on the radio","Reverend Hollis","Nobody's going door to door, they're going room to room in the fellowship hall, and in every room there's a grownup with a piece of hard candy - that's all the holiday there is, I'd say it's enough"}},
{"r5_obit_hollis_all_saints","obituary",114,114,8,12,8,{"Reverend Hollis","Good morning, it's Halloween, and a Sunday, and tomorrow is All Saints' Day - in my church that's the day we read the names of everyone we lost that year","Reverend Hollis","Most years we read nine names, twelve, one bad winter, I have a list here of four hundred and six, and those are only the ones that reached me","Reverend Hollis","I won't read them all on the radio - Marty's generator couldn't stand it, and I don't believe you could either","Reverend Hollis","I'll read one from each town I've heard from, and I'm asking the rest of you to hear your own people in those","Reverend Hollis","From Muldraugh, Hattie Greer, who ran the church nursery, from West Point, Tommy Fuqua, who rang the bell - from Riverside, Nancy Cole, who played the organ too loud and was proud of it","Reverend Hollis","From Rosewood, Earnest Watts, who drove the church van, from March Ridge, Pearl Adkins, who kept the attendance book and knew who was missing before I did","Reverend Hollis","And from every town, the ones nobody sent, I know you're out there - the book is open"}},
{"r3_sf_d115_greer_horse","sign_off",115,150,21,23,1,{"Marty","The days are getting short, we're signing off an hour earlier now, because the generator has to stretch across a lot more dark","Marty","Evening folks. Don't mistake an old road report for an all clear. And whoever called about the Greers' horse, please say when you saw him","Marty","Count your people, check your locks, and put an extra blanket where the dog sleeps - good night"}},
{"r3_wx_d115_wool_over_cotton","weather",115,150,5,10,1,{"Marty","Weather for {weekday}, cold rain all day long, forty-some degrees and gray - the kind of day nobody asks for and November hands out anyway","Marty","Wind out of the northwest, gusting, the leaves are mostly down, you can see a long way into the woods again, which cuts both ways","Marty","If you have to be out in it, wool over cotton - wet cotton is worse than nothing, wet wool still holds some warm in","Dee","And come home before you're soaked through, not after"}},
{"r9_host_d118_santa_letter","host",118,190,0,23,1,{"Marty","Tilford brought me a letter out of the Muldraugh slot, addressed to, and I'm reading it off the envelope, THE MAN ON THE RADIO","Marty","It's from a girl who says she is seven and a half - she wants to know if I'm Santa Claus, her reasoning is that I know everybody's name and I talk at night and nobody's ever seen me","Dee","That's pretty good reasoning","Marty","It's air tight, I've read it four times looking for the hole in it","Marty","So, honey, here's my answer, and your mama can help you with the hard words - I'm not him, I'm a man who sells feed for hogs and chickens, and I'm fifty-six, and I have got a sore back and a cat who hates me","Marty","But I'll tell you what I do know, I know your name now, it's on my board with everybody else's, and that's the best I can do about it"}},
{"r3_so_d120_frost_tracks","sign_on",120,145,6,8,1,{"Marty","Good morning, frost on the lot this morning, the first hard one - the pumpkins nobody picked in the field behind the school have all gone soft and caved in on themselves","Marty","Frost shows tracks, if you go out early, look at the grass before you look at the road - Footprints in frost don't lie about which way somebody went, or how many of them there were","Marty","It's {time}, this is ninety-nine point six - find your gloves"}},
{"r3_host_d120_wanda_not_randall","host",120,150,10,20,1,{"Caller - Wanda","Marty, this is Wanda, you read my note on the radio back in July - the one you couldn't tell if it said Wanda or Randall","Marty","Wanda, it's good to hear you - did you find who you were looking for?","Caller - Wanda","Found my sister, she was at the Methodist church in Rosewood the whole time - took me till October to walk in the door","Caller - Wanda","I only called to tell you my handwriting's gotten better - I've been practicing, in case I ever have to leave another note","Marty","Wanda, I'll have you know I read it right the first time","Caller - Wanda","You did not, you said Randall on the air twice","Marty","Once, it's good to hear you, Wanda"}},
{"r3_ins_d120_winter_water","instruction",120,160,5,12,1,{"Marty","The cold's coming for real now, so let's talk about water in the winter","Marty","A full rain barrel that freezes will split, drain them down to half or bring them inside, same with jugs - leave room at the top of anything you've got water in","Dee","Don't eat snow for water, it costs your body more heat to melt it than the water's worth - melt it on the stove first, and start early, because it takes a lot of snow to make a little water","Marty","If your well pump's electric and dead, a bucket on a rope is your friend - keep the rope inside, or it'll freeze stiff as a board","Dee","Boil it anyway, I've seen a clear bucket put a grown man in bed three days"}},
{"r3_ins_d120_stopped_shivering","instruction",120,160,0,23,1,{"Dee","Hypothermia, it doesn't take snow - it takes wet and wind and forty degrees, and that's most days in November","Dee","First it's shivering hard, then clumsy hands, then mumbling, then they quit shivering and tell you they're fine - when they quit shivering, they're not better - they're worse","Dee","Get them out of the wet clothes, get them into dry blankets, next to somebody warm - something warm to drink if they can hold the cup - not whiskey, whiskey makes you feel warm while it cools you down","Dee","Don't rub their arms and legs hard, and don't put them in hot water - warm the middle first, the chest, the neck, under the arms","Dee","And if you get soaked out there, you go home - I don't care what you were doing, wet and far from home is how I lose people in the winter"}},
{"r3_mb_d120_crow_roost","music_bridge",120,160,0,23,1,{"Marty","In place of a song, I'd like to direct your attention to the crows - there are about two hundred of them in the walnut tree out front, and they've been arguing since daylight","Marty","Nancy used to say a big crow roost in November means a hard winter coming - her daddy said it meant somebody left the corn in the field - this year her daddy was the one who was right","Marty","They're eating well, is what I'm saying, somebody around here is","Marty","Back to it"}},
{"r3_sil_d120_blowing_on_hands","silence",120,160,5,7,1,{"99.6 FM","(The carrier comes on, and for a while there's nothing but someone blowing into their hands)","Marty","Give me a minute, my fingers don't work yet, it's nineteen degrees in this office"}},
{"r5_host_dee_birthday_batteries","host",120,300,0,23,1,{"Dee","Somebody told Marty it was my birthday","Marty","Nobody told me anything, you wrote it on the first page of your chart - you write everything down, Deanna","Dee","He wrapped a pack of D batteries in a feed sack and tied it up with baling twine","Marty","Eight D-cells, that's a flashlight for a month","Dee","I've been given roses by men who meant it a lot less - I'm keeping the sack, everybody"}},
{"r5_trade_tractor_pump_burl","trade",120,300,9,16,1,{"Caller - Burl","Marty, I've got a tractor with a bad hydraulic pump and forty acres I want in corn come spring","Marty","What kind of tractor, Burl?","Caller - Burl","Old diesel, older than my son, I need a pump off anything close, or a man who knows how to rebuild one","Caller - Burl","I'll pay in cornmeal, I've got about three hundred pounds I ground this fall - and I'll pay more cornmeal next fall if the pump holds","Marty","Folks, a man offering next year's crop is a man who plans on being here next year - I like that","Caller - Burl","The Rosewood fire station, at noon, I'll have a sack of meal and the old pump in the truck bed","Marty","Rosewood fire station, noon, a hydraulic pump or a mechanic, for cornmeal now and cornmeal later"}},
}
